<div class="shell layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>What an umpire does</h2>
<p>An umpire only comes into play when the two appraisers have narrowed their differences as far as they can. The umpire reviews the items still in dispute, considers the evidence each appraiser presents, and signs an award with whichever appraiser agrees — or issues a decision on each item that one appraiser then joins. Two signatures make the award binding on the amount of loss.</p>
<h2>How we approach umpire work</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Disclosure first.</strong> We disclose any prior relationship with either appraiser, the carrier or the policyholder before accepting.</li>
<li><strong>Structured submissions.</strong> We ask each appraiser for a list of disputed items with supporting photos, measurements and pricing.</li>
<li><strong>Site inspection where useful.</strong> For roof, structural or matching disputes, seeing the property often resolves questions that photos can’t.</li>
<li><strong>Item-by-item decisions.</strong> Each disputed line receives a decision and a short explanation, which helps the parties understand the outcome.</li>
<li><strong>Clear award language.</strong> Replacement cost, actual cash value and any categories the policy requires are stated plainly.</li>
</ul>
<h2>When panels can’t agree on an umpire</h2>
<p>Many policies say that if the appraisers can’t agree on an umpire within a set period, either side may ask a court of record in the county where the property is located to appoint one. A practical, qualified candidate both appraisers trust avoids that delay and expense.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Neutrality is the whole job</b>When we serve as umpire, we don’t represent the policyholder or the insurer and don’t take an advocate role on that claim. Our services as an appointed appraiser and as an umpire are never combined on the same loss.</div>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who pays the umpire?</summary><div class="a"><p>Most policies split the umpire’s fee equally between the policyholder and the insurer. Each side pays its own appraiser.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can an umpire decide coverage questions?</summary><div class="a"><p>No. The umpire’s role is limited to the amount of loss, the same as the panel’s. Coverage questions stay with the parties and, if needed, the courts.</p></div></details><details><summary>How do appraisers choose an umpire?</summary><div class="a"><p>Usually by exchanging names and agreeing on one. Experience with the type of loss, availability and a record of neutrality matter most.</p></div></details></div>
